lesson: 70
title: Number sense review: tens, ones and how many more
stars: 2
explain: First find each amount: bags of 10 count as tens, loose ones as ones.
explain: Then compare: the one with fewer needs the difference to have as many.
explain: Anna has 4 bags of 10 and 3 loose shells, so 43; Ben has 28. Ben needs 43 - 28 = 15 more.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing c=2..9 o=2..9 m=5..95
need: m < 10 * c + o
answer: 10 * c + o - m
exercise: {a} has {c} bags of 10 {t} and {o} loose {t}. {b} has {m} {t}. How many more {t} does {b} need to have as many as {a}?
exercise: {b} has {m} {t}. {a} has {c} boxes of ten {t} and {o} more. How many more {t} must {b} get to match {a}?
exercise: The class needs {c} packs of 10 pencils and {o} more pencils. The cupboard has {m} pencils. How many more pencils does the class have to get?
exercise: {a} tied {c} bundles of 10 sticks and has {o} single sticks. {b} has only {m} sticks. How many sticks short is {b}?
exercise: {a} saved {c} ten-dollar notes and {o} one-dollar coins. {b} saved {m} dollars. How many more dollars must {b} save to have as much as {a}?
test: {b} owns {m} {t}; {a} owns {o} loose ones and {c} full packs of ten. How many must {b} add to own the same number? ({a} also has 3 empty packs.)
test: What must be added to {m} to make the number with {c} tens and {o} ones?
test: A farmer packs {c} trays of 10 eggs and has {o} eggs on the side. Another farmer has {m} eggs and 2 baskets. How many more eggs does the second farmer need to have as many eggs?
test: {a} reads {c} ten-page chapters and {o} more pages; {b} reads {m} pages. By how many pages must {b} read on to catch up?
exercise: {a} has {c} packets of ten {t} and {o} single {t}. {b} has {m} {t}. How many more {t} must {b} collect to have just as many?
exercise: How many more {t} does {b} need to match {a}? {a} has {c} bags of ten and {o} loose {t}; {b} has {m} {t}.
exercise: A school in Ohio has {c} boxes of 10 rulers and {o} loose rulers. Another school has {m} rulers. How many more rulers does the second school need to have as many?
exercise: {b} has {m} dollars. {a} has {c} ten-dollar bills and {o} one-dollar bills. How many more dollars does {b} need to have as much as {a}?
exercise: In a Scottish orchard, {a} fills {c} crates of 10 apples and has {o} apples left on the grass. {b} picks {m} apples. How many more apples must {b} pick to equal {a}?
exercise: {a}'s number has {c} tens and {o} ones. {b}'s number is {m}. How much must be added to {b}'s number to make {a}'s?
exercise: {a} has {c.words} bundles of ten sticks and {o.words} loose sticks. {b} has {m} sticks. How many sticks is {b} short?
exercise: A shop in Perth has {c} trays of 10 cupcakes and {o} cupcakes on a plate. Another shop has {m} cupcakes. How many more cupcakes does the second shop need to have the same number?
test: In Halifax, {a} stacks {c} piles of ten coins and puts {o} coins on the side, while {b} has {m} coins in a jar and 2 empty jars. How many coins must {b} add to have as many as {a}?
test: How far is {m} from the number made of {c} tens and {o} ones?
test: {b} has {m} {t}. {a} has {o} single {t} plus {c} full tens. By how many {t} does {b} fall short?
test: A library in Belfast receives {c} boxes holding 10 books each, plus {o} books in a bag. A second library, which has 4 shelves, receives {m} books. How many more books would the second library need to equal the first?
test: Equal amounts are wanted: {a} owns {c} bundles of ten {t} and {o} more; {b} owns {m}. How many does {b} still lack?
test: {a} bought {c} packs of 10 crayons and {o} single crayons for 5 dollars. {b} has {m} crayons. How many more crayons would {b} need for the two to have the same number?
exercise: {a}'s got {c} bags of 10 {t} n {o} loose, {b}'s got {m}. how many more {b} needs to match
exercise: class needs {c} packs of 10 pencils + {o}. cupboard's got {m}. how many more we get
exercise: {b} has {m+1} {t} — no wait, {m} — and {a} has {c} tens and {o} ones. how many more for {b} to catch up
exercise: Kai says {b} needs {10*c+o+m} more. nah, {b}'s got {m} and {a}'s got {c} tens {o} ones. how many really
test: {c} boxes of ten {t} plus {o} extra for {a}, {b}'s on {m}. how many more {b} gotta get to have as many
test: {m} {t} at {b}'s, {a}'s got {c} tens n {o}, oh and its {b}'s bday on the 5th. how many more does {b} need to match
test: {a} {c} tens {o} ones of {t}, {b} just {m}. gap between em?
exam: Whereas {a} holds {c} packets of ten {t} together with {o} loose {t}, and {b} holds {m} {t}, determine the number of {t} which {b} must acquire in order to equal {a}.
exam: Ascertain the difference between the number composed of {c} tens and {o} units and the number {m}.
exam: It is given that {b} possesses {m.words} {t}. State the deficiency of {b}'s holding in respect of {c.words} bundles of ten {t} and {o.words} further {t}.
exam: Compute the quantity by which {m} falls short of {c} tens and {o} ones.
exercise: When Grandpa passed away, {a} got {c} ten-dollar notes and {o} one-dollar coins from him. {b} has {m} dollars. How many more dollars does {b} need to have as much as {a}?
exercise: The food bank had {c} boxes of 10 tins and {o} loose tins. It gave {m} tins to families who have 'fallen on hard times'. How many tins are left?
test: {b}'s 'temporarily embarrassed' lol, skint basically. got {m} quid. {a}'s got {c} tenners and {o} quid in coins. how much more does {b} need to match {a}, for crying out loud
